{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Alexander's witness is a fixed machine index m: the same membership test ⌜φ⌝ ∈ W_m occurs in every instance. Lean preserves the outer K and uses one F for all φ, but F is an arbitrary function on sentences, with no index, effectiveness, or uniform defining formula required. Even taking F = K makes the inner biconditionals tautological. The scheme therefore need not express knowledge of a code or of coincidence with a particular machine in Koellner's sense."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Alexander asserts satisfaction of the axioms of epistemic arithmetic mod factivity in M_Σ(n), with the schema ranging over sentences of the epistemic language. Lean instead asserts provability in a consistent theory of ordinary arithmetic, with K acting externally on closed sentences. Its selected proof conditions are not the model-satisfaction claim; in particular, Def1 requires PA itself to prove Kφ whenever PA proves φ."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean requires a witness ψ for which PA proves ψ ↔ ¬F(ψ). The passages do not state this fixed-point clause. For an arithmetically defined membership test one can appeal to diagonalization, but for the arbitrary sentence map in Lean this is a substantive separate requirement."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean also asserts that for some p, the theory proves K(K(¬p) ↔ p), with the entire biconditional known. No quoted passage asserts such a sentence; it is an additional conclusion, not an instance of Alexander's machine-index schema."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "‘Containing PA’ is expressed as inclusion of provable sentences, not literal inclusion of PA's axioms in the witnessing axiom set. This suffices for reasoning from PA theorems."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target matches the advertised abstract conjunction and does leave known factivity out as a hypothesis. As a statement of the source result, however, it replaces the indexed machine and epistemic structure with unconstrained sentence operators and a proof theory, and adds two fixed-point-related claims."
}
